<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The change from Washington&rsquo;s Birthday to <strong>Presidents&rsquo; Day</strong> began in the late 1960s, when Congress proposed a measure known as the Uniform Monday Holiday Act. Leaded by Senator Robert McClory of Illinois, this law offered to
    shift the celebration of several federal holidays from specific dates to a series of predetermined Mondays. The Uniform Monday Holiday Act included a provision to combine the celebration of Washington&rsquo;s birthday with that of Abraham Lincoln,
    which fell on February 12. Lincoln&rsquo;s Birthday had been celebrated as a state holiday in places like Illinois. Many people supported joining two days by considering it as a way of giving equal recognition to two of America&rsquo;s most famous
    figures.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<p>The date change from February 22 to third Monday of February made many citizens believe that the new date was intended to honor both Washington and Lincoln, as it now fell between their two birthdays. Then the marketers jumped at the opportunity to play
    up the three-day weekend with sales, and &ldquo;<strong>Presidents&rsquo; Day</strong>&rdquo; name were advertised at stores around the country. By the mid-1980s, Washington&rsquo;s Birthday was already known to many Americans as <strong>Presidents&rsquo; Day</strong>.
